  <ul>
    <li><strong>One airline's entry rules (IndiGo's careers page):</strong> women who are Indian nationals with an Indian passport, aged 18 to 27, with 10+2 from a recognised board and a minimum height of 155 cm, weight in proportion to BMI, and fluency in English and Hindi. Male hiring is not clearly stated, so check the current notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Training:</strong> a third-party page says new crew train for about two to three months at the airline's own academy and that the airline pays for training. Treat that as unconfirmed and read the offer.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical:</strong> DGCA issues a requirement under Rule 38B of the Aircraft Rules, 1937, that operators keep each crew member medically fit. Press reports describe an examination equal to a Class 2 medical at joining and repeat checks through life, and a BMI rule that can end flying duties if not met in time.</li>
    <li><strong>Shelf life:</strong> an entry age cap, medical rules and a fixed ladder mean the flying years are limited. Plan what you do after.</li>
