  <h2 id="drones">Drone pilot and drone services</h2>

  <p>
    Drones are the lowest-cost door in aviation. Under the Drone Rules, 2021, a remote pilot certificate is for people aged 18 to 65 who have passed Class 10 and completed training at a DGCA-authorised remote pilot training organisation. The Drone (Amendment) Rules, 2022 removed the separate pilot licence step, and the certificate is valid for ten years while it stays on the Digital Sky platform.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What a government scheme shows</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>The Namo Drone Didi scheme plans drones for 15,000 women self-help groups from 2024-25 to 2025-26, with help of 80% of the drone cost up to Rs 8 lakh.</li>
      <li>One member per group gets a 15-day course: 5 days of drone pilot training and 10 days on crop spraying. PIB expects at least Rs 1 lakh extra income per group a year. That is a target for groups, not a salary.</li>
      <li>We did not check whether the scheme was extended past 2025-26, so verify before you plan around it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      The lesson is the model: a drone earns when it is tied to a paid job, such as spraying, mapping or inspection. A certificate alone does not bring clients.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start with one use that has a clear payer. Learn the rules for flying zones and Digital Sky registration, keep records of every job, and check current GST and local rules on the official portals before you charge clients.
  </p>

