  <h2 id="scams">Scam warning: placement and finishing-school promises</h2>

  <p>
    Aviation jobs attract fake recruiters because many people dream of them. A real airline hires you and does not sell you the job.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">A real case and the pattern</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Delhi, August 2026: police arrested six people behind a fake airline, "Bretish Airways". Instagram ads promised airport jobs, callers posing as HR told applicants they needed a course, and victims paid Rs 7,000 to Rs 10,000 as admission fees. The group changed names after complaints.</li>
      <li>IndiGo's careers page warns that people misuse its name to ask for money for interviews or jobs, and says it does not charge for recruitment.</li>
      <li>Red flags: a job found through a social ad, a fee, a "mandatory" course, documents sent on WhatsApp, an airline name you cannot find on its own site, and pressure to pay fast.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      Never pay for registration, uniforms, interviews or an offer letter. Apply only from the airline's own careers page. Report fraud on 1930 or the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal.
    </p>
  </div>

